{
 "scheme": {
  "summary": "Ceps_cert = -eps * (certified lower bound on nu_1), where nu_1 = lambda_min(B) and B := A_eps/eps = -Lap + V/eps on H^1(Omega) with Neumann conditions.",
  "why_admissible": "The projection bound (thm:coulomb-bound) is applied to B, whose Laplacian coefficient is normalised to 1 -- which is what the theorem assumes. The preliminary form bound at any alpha in (0,eps) is divided through by eps, giving eps_star = alpha/eps in (0,1) and C_star = sigma_loc(alpha)/eps, so the budget 1 - eps_star is O(1) and does NOT collapse. Failing to normalise before applying the bound is what made the analytic constant look optimal.",
  "contrast_with_v0": "The ORIGINAL shift Ceps = -eta_{1,N} is INADMISSIBLE: the cosine space is a subspace of H^1(Omega), so Rayleigh-Ritz bounds eta from ABOVE, eta_{1,N} >= eta, hence -eta_{1,N} <= Ceps^opt -- it sits BELOW the quantity it must bound. The scheme here uses a VERIFIED ENCLOSURE of eta_{1,N} as INPUT to the projection bound rather than as the shift, which is what restores admissibility.",
  "identity_used": "nu_{1,N} = eta_{1,N}/eps EXACTLY: same trial space, operator scaled by 1/eps. So the existing H_aux = eps*K + P verified enclosure divides straight through. Verified numerically at every row (identity_eps_nu1N_encloses_eta1N).",
  "alpha_and_delta": "alpha is free -- EVERY alpha in (0,eps) gives a valid bound -- so it is optimised in float and the single chosen alpha is then certified in interval arithmetic, costing nothing in rigour. delta is free too and has a closed-form optimum: since (1+1/delta)/(1+delta) = 1/delta identically, sigma_loc(alpha,delta) = Z^2(1+delta)/(4 alpha) + alpha/((r2-r1)^2 delta) + Z/r1, minimised at delta = 2 alpha/(Z (r2-r1)), giving sigma_loc^opt(alpha) = Z^2/(4 alpha) + Z/(r2-r1) + Z/r1. On Omega_1 (Z=2, r1=4, r2=8) that is exactly 1/alpha + 1, and it reproduces the author's pairs: alpha=0.4 -> delta=1/10 -> 7/2, alpha=0.3 -> delta=3/40 -> 13/3. The author's (alpha,delta) choices were already delta-optimal.",
  "sector_justification": "Step 2 uses the eee block, and that is a THEOREM here, not an assumption: B commutes with the three coordinate reflections; its Neumann ground state on a connected domain is unique and strictly positive (positivity-improving semigroup); a reflection maps the one-dimensional ground eigenspace to itself so Ru = +-u; and u > 0 excludes the minus sign. Hence the ground state is even under all three reflections. Unlike the lambda_2 sector assignment this needs no hypothesis.",
  "standing_caveat": "sharp_L_iv downstream uses the form factor etaV = sqrt(e/(1-e) + Ceps/ms1), not the manuscript's proved etaV = Ztot*(C_grad/(1-e) + C_L2/ms1)^{1/2}. With the proved form the separation certificate would need N ~ 186, out of reach. These runs therefore carry exactly the same conditional status as the published ones. Not switched."
 },
